  "explanation": {
    "markdown": "## **Most, most of, the most** : Grammar chart\n\n![Educational infographic illustrating the grammatical differences between 'most', 'most of', and 'the most' with examples for each category.](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-1.png)\n\n### **Most**\n\nWe use **most** (without **the/this/that/my/etc.**) with nouns to mean ‘the majority of’ something.\n\n-   ***Most of people** can’t work from home. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-2.png)* \n-   ***Most people** can’t work from home. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-3.png)* \n-   *I have some bad days, but **most days** are good.* \n\nWe can use **most** + noun to talk about the majority of people or things in general\n\n-   ***Most students** like online learning.*\n\n### **Most of**\n\nWe use **most of** before **the/this/that/my/etc.**\n\n-   ***Most of students** in my class practice sports. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-4.png)* \n-   ***Most of the students** in my class practice sports. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-5.png)* \n-   ***Most of this information** is not true.* \n\nWe can also use **most of** + [object pronoun](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/object-pronouns/).\n\n-   ***Most of us** come from poor families.* \n-   *They arrested **most of them**.*\n\n#### Be careful with this common mistake!\n\nWe say **most** or **most of** (NOT **the most**) to talk about the majority of something.\n\n-   ***The most students** like online learning. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-6.png)* \n-   ***Most students** like online learning. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-7.png)* \n-   ***The most of us** come from poor families. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-8.png)* \n-   ***Most of us** come from poor families. ![](/uploads/grammar/a2/most-most-of-the-most/img-9.png)* \n\n### **The most**\n\nWe say the most before [adjectives or adverbs](https://test-english.com/grammar-points/a1/adverbs-manner/) to make the superlative form.\n\n-   *These are **the most comfortable** sofas you can buy.* \n-   *Here, you can find **the most beautiful** views in the region.*",
